<section class="section section--alt about">
  <div class="wrap about__layout">
    <div class="section-heading about__intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h2>Hey, I'm Shanthi.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="about__photo" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="about__photo-frame">
        <span>Photo of Shanthi<br />(coming soon)</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="about__body">
      <p>
        I was standing next to the CEO when we scaled from 5 people to 60, right after
        we closed a $14M Series A.
      </p>

      <p>
        I learned how not to waste resources — where to put your budget, where not to,
        which marketing bets to make first and when to scale them, and how to hire
        without burning either money or morale.
      </p>

      <p>
        The hardest part isn't the growth itself — it's that what your leadership team
        needs at 5 people is completely different from what it needs at 100. Past a
        certain size, your leaders can't keep a personal pulse on everything anymore, so
        your systems have to do that work instead: real-time, visible to everyone,
        understood across every team. Skip that work and the cost is real — wasted
        money, lost morale, and hires made too fast or in the wrong seats, when your
        people are the foundation the whole company sits on.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Heart and rigor.</strong> Before startups, I did this same kind of work
        in global health — two $20M programs launched across Sub-Saharan Africa, a
        $300M portfolio managed across 15+ countries, systems built inside a refugee
        camp in Bangladesh. When a broken process means someone doesn't get care, you
        learn to take the details seriously. I bring that same standard to your
        company.
      </p>

      <p class="about__closing">I've done this twice now. I'd like to do it for you next.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
